We now combine ERoPE, integration over \(S^{2}\) and ideas from linear-scaling attention to arrive at EFA. It is given by
\[
\mathrm{EFA}(\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{R})_{m}=\frac{1}{4 \pi} \int_{S^{2}} \boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\mathbf{u}}\left(\mathbf{q}_{m}, \mathbf{r}_{m}\right)^{\top} \sum_{n=1}^{N} \overline{\boldsymbol{\Phi}_{\mathbf{u}}\left(\mathbf{k}_{n}, \mathbf{r}_{n}\right)} \mathbf{v}_{n}^{\top} \mathrm{d} \mathbf{u},
\]
where we define the short-hand \(\phi_{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r}):=\mathrm{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}(\Psi(\mathbf{x}), \mathbf{r})\) for conciseness, and \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}\) can be any feature map (see Supplementary equation (5)). We base EFA on an attention-like mechanism given by
\[
\widetilde{\mathrm{ATT}}_{\mathrm{Lin}}(X)_{m}=\boldsymbol{\Psi}\left(\mathbf{q}_{m}\right)^{\mathrm{T}} \sum_{n=1}^{N} \boldsymbol{\Psi}\left(\mathbf{k}_{n}\right) \mathbf{v}_{n}^{\mathrm{T}},
\]
which omits the denominator compared to standard attention formulations (see Supplementary equation (5)). Omitting normalization is natural when modelling long-range interactions in chemical systems because their effects are typically additive (size extensive).

