where \(\mathbf{f}\) is a vector-valued radial function and ' ' ' denotes element-wise multiplication (see 'Analytic solution of the surface integral' section in Supplementary Information). Equation (6) resembles the structure of SO(3) convolutions-the basic MP building block in many equivariant MPNNs \({ }^{30,42}\)-but now without needing to introduce a local cutoff to achieve linear scaling. In the 'Extended scaling analysis' section in Supplementary Information, we analyse the error of the EFA kernel function as a function of system size, to empirically validate linear scaling in \(N\).

\section*{Idealized systems}

Before applying EFA to realistic molecular systems and materials, we evaluate and contrast it with standard MP on idealized model systems.

Geometric expressiveness. First, we investigate the geometric expressiveness of EFA and MP, that is, their ability to resolve geometric information. When distinguishing increasingly complex pairs of local atomic neighbourhoods \({ }^{43}\), we empirically confirm the similarity between EFA and \(\mathrm{SO}(3)\) convolutions and find that increasing the degree \(\ell\) in \(\mathbf{Y}_{\ell}\) has the same effect for both operations (Fig. 2a; Methods).

To analyse their ability to distinguish molecular graphs as a whole, we test whether MP and EFA can distinguish two graphs that are non-isomorphic according to a geometric Weisfeiler-Leman \({ }^{44,45}\) test (Fig. 2b). Unlike a standard MP model, which requires an increasing number of layers to distinguish the non-isomorphic graphs, EFA accomplishes this within a single update by capturing global structural information.

Pairwise potentials. Next, we consider pairwise potentials, such that the total energy of a system of \(N\) atoms is given by
\[
E\left(\mathbf{r}_{1}, \ldots, \mathbf{r}_{N}\right)=\sum_{m}^{N} \sum_{n>m}^{N} V\left(\left\|\mathbf{r}_{m}-\mathbf{r}_{n}\right\|\right),
\]
where \(V\) is a scalar potential function.
Two-particle systems. We start with the simplest case of two particles ( \(N=2\) ) and choose \(V\) such that the long-range decay behaviour is reminiscent of (attractive) charge-charge interactions (Methods). As expected, a standard MPNN predicts a qualitatively wrong, constant energy profile as soon as the particle separation exceeds the cutoff distance. When the model is augmented with EFA, the pairwise potential can be accurately described over the full interaction length (Fig.1a).

