To reveal the shortcomings of purely distance-based descriptions and show the utility of the equivariant version of the EFA mechanism (equation (5)), the potential \(V\) is made to mimic a non-isotropic chargedipole interaction, that is, it now not only depends on distance, but also orientation (Methods). Only EFA models with \(\ell \geq 1\) in the spherical harmonics vector \(\mathbf{Y}_{\ell}\) capture the true nature of the underlying potential over all length scales (Fig. 2c).
\(N\)-particle systems. Next we consider increasingly large NaCl-like systems (Fig. 3a), where negatively charged Cl and positively charged Na ions interact via a screened Coulomb potential (Methods).

For each system, we train a single-layer MP model with and without an EFA block. We find that using EFA strongly improves the accuracy (Fig. 3c) and scales linearly with the number of atoms (Fig. 3d). We further show that a modified, minimal EFA variant can be trained on two-particle NaCl -like systems and afterwards be applied to much larger systems ( \(>10,000\) atoms), while maintaining a constant error per atom (see 'Extended scaling analysis' section in Supplementary Information).

We further compare MP+EFA with MPs with an increasing number of layers, such that the effective cutoff covers the whole system (Fig. 3b). Our results suggest that 'mean-field interactions' between node neighbourhoods in MPNNs do not capture all relevant details of interatomic potentials, and augmenting models with EFA is still beneficial in this setting.

\section*{Molecular systems and materials}

In the following, we investigate the performance of EFA on realistic data. We study several atomistic systems exhibiting various long-ranged and non-local effects, which are representative for common interaction
patterns found throughout chemistry. We demonstrate that EFA systematically improves the performance of local MPNNs and that an accurate description of long-range interactions can be crucial for predicting the dynamic properties of molecular systems.

Non-local charge transfer. Ko et al. proposed a benchmark for analysing the capability of MLFFs to model non-local charge transfer effects \({ }^{46}\). The test systems include molecules and materials, requiring applicability to systems with and without PBCs. For each system, we train a regular MPNN and an EFA-augmented variant (Methods) and compare energy and force errors with other state-of-the-art methods (Table 1). For the standard MPNN we see increased errors, whereas the model with EFA outperforms all other methods in seven out of eight metrics.
\(\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{N}} \mathbf{2}\) reactions. As an example of chemical reactions, we focus on prototypical \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) reactions of the type \(\mathrm{X}^{-}+\mathrm{H}_{3} \mathrm{C}-\mathrm{Y} \rightarrow \mathrm{X}-\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\mathrm{Y}^{-}\), where X and Y are halogens ( \(\mathrm{F}, \mathrm{Cl}, \mathrm{Br}\) or I) \({ }^{47}\). We find that adding an EFA block to a local MPNN architecture ( \(r_{\text {cut }}=5 \AA\) ) yields a \(34 \times\) and \(8 \times\) reduction in mean absolute errors for energy and force predictions, respectively (Supplementary Table 1). Merely increasing the local cutoff to \(r_{\text {cut }}= 10 \AA\) or including dispersion corrections does not yield satisfactory improvements (Supplementary Fig. 9 and Supplementary Table 1). The reason for the substantially lower mean absolute errors when using EFA becomes apparent when visualizing one-dimensional cuts of the potential energy surface along the reaction coordinate (Fig. 4a). Both models without EFA predict the wrong asymptotic behaviour, whereas the model with EFA accurately describes the energy profile for the full range of particle separations. The unphysical artefacts in the long-range region of the potential energy surface predicted by the standard MP

