Dimers. Next, we use EFA for non-covalent interactions of dimer systems sampled from the challenging DES370K benchmark \({ }^{48}\). To perform well on this benchmark, ML models need to generalize across the functional form of different long-range interactions (for example, electrostatics, induction, dispersion or mixtures thereof) and molecular structures. Consistent with our previous findings, MP fails to model dimer interaction energies as soon as the separation between the individual molecules exceeds the cutoff, whereas MP+EFA captures the energy profile faithfully (Fig. 4c). We also find robust generalization of the MP+EFA model to four completely unseen dimers (Supplementary Fig. 12a).

To further quantify the agreement of different models with respect to the ground-truth reference, we fit the long-range behaviour of the intermolecular interactions to the functional form
\[
V_{\mathrm{lr}}\left(r_{A B}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{6} c_{i} r_{A B}^{-i},
\]
where \(c_{i}\) are expansion coefficients and \(r_{A B}\) is the distance between molecules \(A\) and \(B\). Equation (8) can capture the behaviour of many physically relevant interaction types, for example, charge-charge ( \(r_{A B}^{-1}\) ), charge-dipole ( \(r_{A B}^{-2}\) ) or dispersion ( \(r_{A B}^{-6}\) ). We calculate the Pearson correlation \(s\) of coefficients for model predictions with those for the ground truth and find \(s=0.56\) for the standard MP model versus \(s=\) 0.95 for the MP+EFA model. Furthermore, we find excellent agreement between the coefficient distribution for the ground truth and the MP+EFA model, whereas the MP model yields a qualitatively wrong distribution (see Fig. 4d and Supplementary Fig. 12c for individual \(c_{i}\) ).

Electronic delocalization. So far, our analyses have focused primarily on long-range interactions that can be described as functions of atomic (or molecular) distances \(r\), usually decaying proportionally to some power law \(r^{-b}\). However, some chemical systems exhibit non-local effects, which are more complicated in nature and cannot easily be described as a function of distance. Instead, they are usually characterized by a strong dependence of the energy on the relative orientation of substructures in a molecule. Here, we consider the non-local effects in cumulene molecules, which have been found to be particularly challenging for both global and local MLFFs \({ }^{3,40}\).

In cumulenes, the energy strongly depends on the dihedral angle \(\Theta\) between the hydrogen rotors at the ends of a chain of carbon atoms (Fig.5a). We compare a \(T=3\) layer MPNN augmented with three EFA versions to regular MPNNs with \(T=3\) and \(T=5\) layers. \(\mathrm{EFA}_{\ell=0}\) uses only invariant features, whereas \(\mathrm{EFA}_{\ell=1}\) and \(\mathrm{EFA}_{\ell=2}\) use equivariant representations up to degree \(\ell=1\) and \(\ell=2\). The MP \(+\mathrm{EFA}_{\ell=0}\) model cannot describe the energy profile, because it leverages only pairwise distances, which cannot resolve the change in the dihedral angle sufficiently well \({ }^{40}\).

As soon as equivariant representations are allowed to enter the EFA update ( \(\ell>0\) ), the energy is modelled faithfully (higher degrees enable more accurate energy barrier predictions). MP models without EFA fail and predict a flat energy profile as soon as the distance between hydrogen rotors exceeds the effective cutoff. Even when the effective cutoff is large enough ( \(T=5\) ), the barrier height is still underestimated (Fig. 5a, inset), indicating over-squashing of information \({ }^{49}\). Modelling the energy barrier correctly is crucially important for predicting the correct dynamical behaviour during MD simulations. For example, models that predict a flat energy profile incorrectly sample all possible dihedral angles between the terminal \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) rotors as equally likely (Fig. 5b), which leads to wrong predictions of physical observables (Fig. 5c). We note that all tested models are unable to reproduce the sharp cusp at a dihedral angle of \(180^{\circ}\), which is an artefact of the reference ab initio method (see 'Cusp in cumulene' section in Supplementary Information).

Systems without strong long-range effects. For completeness, we evaluate standard MPNNs and EFA-augmented versions on the materials investigated in ref. 50, which are dominated by local interactions. As expected, because these systems exhibit no strong long-range effects, both MP and MP+EFA models perform comparably (Supplementary Table 3). Similar results can be observed on standard molecular benchmarks where local interactions dominate (see 'Molecular benchmarks dominated by local interactions' section in Supplementary Information).

\section*{Discussion and conclusion}

For computational efficiency, many MLFFs use a local cutoff, which (by design) prevents modelling global and long-range interactions. While global interactions are often weaker than short-range interactions, they are crucial for accurately modelling large systems such as proteins and solids \({ }^{16}\). Developing methods to faithfully treat these global effects is therefore essential for scaling MLFFs to larger, chemically relevant systems.

Different approaches have been proposed to overcome the limitations of strictly local MLFFs. They can be broadly categorized into two classes: methods that (1) augment otherwise local models with global correction terms or (2) abandon the concept of strict locality altogether by instead learning global representations. The first category typically relies on a physically motivated description of specific long-range effects \({ }^{29,46,47,51-58}\). These approaches usually involve learning intermediate, local quantities, which are then used as parameters in fixed interaction terms. The computational complexity of naively evaluating these terms typically scales quadratically with the number of atoms, but can be sped up with techniques such as the fast multipole method \({ }^{59}\), which allows their application to large systems. A popular example for this kind of approach is to predict partial charges, either directly \({ }^{29,47,53,54}\) or via atomic electronegativities, which are used as inputs to a charge equilibration scheme (to account for non-local charge transfer effects) \({ }^{46}\). The obtained charges can then be used to
