\section*{Methods}

\section*{EFA}

In the following, we describe only the implementation of EFA in its most general form (equation (5)), which assumes equivariant features (the mechanism for invariant features in equation (3) can be recovered as a special case). We first describe all individual components and then show how they are combined to the full EFA mechanism.

Equivariant features. On a high level, equivariant features can be thought of as containing additional 'directional information'. Under transformations of the coordinate system, the numerical values of equivariant features may change in a complicated manner, but they still
encode 'the same' directional information (but transformed accordingly). Here, we briefly describe a particular type of equivariant features introduced in ref. 41.

The equivariant features we consider consist of irreducible representations (irreps) of the orthogonal group in three dimensions \(\mathrm{O}(3)\) (rotations and reflections). Features of degree \(\ell\) are denoted as \(\mathbf{x}^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times(2 \ell+1) \times H}\), where \(P\) is the size of the 'parity axis' (either 1 or 2) and \(H\) is the feature space dimension. The parity of an irrep can be either even ( +1 ) or odd ( -1 ) and determines how it behaves under reflections (it either changes sign or not), whereas the degree \(\ell\) determines the behaviour under rotations (an irrep of degree \(\ell\) has \(2 \ell+1\) components/ is described by \(2 \ell+1\) numbers). For example, irreps with \(\ell=0\) are described by a single number and do not change (are invariant) under rotations, whereas irreps with \(\ell=1\) consist of three components and rotate similar to ordinary three-dimensional vectors. When irreps have parity +1 for even \(\ell\) and -1 for odd \(\ell\), we refer to them as 'tensors', and when they have the opposite parity, we call them 'pseudotensors'. Features either consist only of tensors (in which case \(P=1\) ), or of both tensors and pseudotensors (in which case \(P=2\) and irreps of even/odd parity are stored in separate slices, that is, indices 0 and 1 , of the parity axis). For a more detailed overview, we refer the reader to ref. 41.

We are usually working with the concatenation of features starting from the lowest degree \(\ell=0\) up to some maximum degree \(L\), which we denote as \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times(L+1)^{2} \times H}\) (each degree \(\ell\) contributes a slice of size \(2 \ell+1\), giving a total size \((L+1)^{2}\) for the 'degree axis'). We use \(\mathbf{x}_{m}\) to refer to the
representation of the \(m\) th atom and \(\mathbf{x}^{\left(\ell_{ \pm}\right)}\)to refer to the 'slice' of irreps of degree \(\ell\) with parity \(p= \pm 1\). Invariant features, for example those used in SchNet \({ }^{9}\) or PhysNet \({ }^{47}\), can be considered as a special case with \(L=0\) and \(P=1\), that is, they only consist of irreps with degree 0 and even parity.

Two irrep representations \(\mathbf{x}\) and \(\mathbf{y}\) can be 'coupled' via tensor product contractions \({ }^{66}\) to produce new features \(\mathbf{z}\). The irreps of degree \(c\) and parity \(\gamma\) of the new features \(\mathbf{z}\) are given by
\[
\mathbf{z}^{\left(c_{\gamma}\right)}=\sum_{\left(a_{\alpha}, b_{\beta}\right)} \mathbf{x}^{\left(a_{\alpha}\right)} \otimes^{\left(c_{\gamma}\right)} \mathbf{y}^{\left(b_{\beta}\right)}
\]
where the sum runs over all combinations of irreps with degrees \(a+b =c\) and parities \(\alpha \times \beta=\gamma\) of the input features \(\mathbf{x}\) and \(\mathbf{y}\). Evaluating the ‘ \(\otimes^{\left(c_{\gamma}\right) \text { ) }}\) operation in equation (9) involves a summation over components of the tensor (outer) product weighed with so-called Clebsch-Gordan coefficients (see ref. 41 for implementation details).

Performing all possible tensor product contractions between irreps of maximal degree \(L_{x}\) and \(L_{y}\) up to a maximal output degree \(L_{z} \leq L_{x} +L_{y}\) is written as
\[
\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{x} \bigotimes_{L_{z}}^{L_{x} L_{y}} \mathbf{y},
\]
such that \(\mathbf{z} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times\left(L_{z}+1\right)^{2} \times H}\). In other words, this is just a compact notation for the concatenation of all tensor product contractions (equation (9)) for \(c=0, \ldots, L_{z}\) and \(\gamma= \pm 1\).

EROPE. Following typical implementations of the RoPE mechanism \({ }^{37}\), EROPE is implemented in a slightly different (but mathematically equivalent) manner than what is suggested in equation (1). Consider a complex number \(c=a+b i(c \in \mathbb{C}\) and \(a, b \in \mathbb{R})\). Recall that, to encode a position \(\mathbf{r} \in \mathbb{R}^{3}\) in Euclidean space into \(c\) with ERoPE, we calculate
\[
\mathrm{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}(c, \mathbf{r})=c \times \mathrm{e}^{i \omega \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}} .
\]

To avoid computations with complex numbers, we can instead collect the real and imaginary parts of \(c\) into a vector \(\mathbf{x}=[a b]^{T} \in \mathbb{R}^{2}\) and perform an equivalent encoding of \(\mathbf{r}\) into \(\mathbf{x}\) as
\[
\operatorname{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r})=\mathbf{M} \mathbf{x},
\]
where \(\mathbf{M}\) is the \(2 \times 2\) rotation matrix
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
\cos (\omega \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}) & -\sin (\omega \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}) \\
\sin (\omega \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}) & \cos (\omega \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r})
\end{array}\right] .
\]

This formulation can be extended to higher-dimensional vectors \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{H}\) with \(H=2 K\), where \(\mathbf{M}\) is now a \((2 K) \times(2 K)\) block-diagonal matrix consisting of \(2 \times 2\) rotation matrices and we allow different coefficients \(\omega_{k}\) for each of the \(K\) rotations matrices (the motivation for this is explained below). For high-dimensional vectors \(\mathbf{x}\), instead of evaluating \(\mathbf{M x}\) via matrix multiplication, it is more efficient (due to the sparsity of \(\mathbf{M}\) ) to compute
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r})= & {\left[\begin{array}{l}
\cos \left(\omega_{1} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\cos \left(\omega_{1} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\vdots \\
\cos \left(\omega_{K} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\cos \left(\omega_{K} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right)
\end{array}\right] \odot\left[\begin{array}{l}
x_{1} \\
x_{2} \\
\vdots \\
x_{2 K-1} \\
x_{2 K}
\end{array}\right] } \\
& +\left[\begin{array}{l}
\sin \left(\omega_{1} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\sin \left(\omega_{1} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\vdots \\
\sin \left(\omega_{K} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right) \\
\sin \left(\omega_{K} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}\right)
\end{array}\right] \odot\left[\begin{array}{l}
-x_{2} \\
x_{1} \\
\vdots \\
-x_{2 K} \\
x_{2 K-1}
\end{array}\right],
\end{aligned}
\]
where ' \(\odot\) ' denotes element-wise multiplication and \(x_{j}\) is the \(j\) th entry of \(\mathbf{x}\). For vectors with an odd number of dimensions, that is, \(H=2 K-1\),
the missing entry \(x_{2 K}\) can be simply replaced by zero ( \(\mathbf{x}\) is zero-padded to an even number of dimensions).

Expressed in words, if we interpret a vector \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 K}\) as consisting of the concatenated real and imaginary parts of the (complex) entries of a vector \(\tilde{\mathbf{x}} \in \mathbb{C}^{K}\), then applying equation (11) to \(\mathbf{x}\) (with \(\omega_{k}=\omega\) ) is equivalent to applying equation (1) to \(\widetilde{\mathbf{x}}\). The motivation for allowing different \(\omega_{k}\) for each entry \(k\) is that it increases the expressivity of the operation: recall that after integration over \(S^{2}\), encoding positions \(\mathbf{r}_{m}\) and \(\mathbf{r}_{n}\) into vectors \(\mathbf{q}_{m}\) and \(\mathbf{k}_{n}\) with ERoPE and computing their scalar product essentially corresponds to scaling \(\left\langle\mathbf{q}_{m}, \mathbf{k}_{n}\right\rangle\) with \(\operatorname{sinc}\left(\omega r_{m n}\right)\) (see equation (2) and 'Analytic solution of the surface integral' section in Supplementary Information). When different \(\omega_{k}\) are used for each entry, instead of applying a uniform scaling factor, the contribution of different entries to the scalar product can be scaled independently by factors of \(\operatorname{sinc}\left(\omega_{k} r_{m n}\right)\), which allows one to express a more complicated radial dependence.

The extension of ERoPE to equivariant features \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times(L+1)^{2} \times H}\) is straightforward: we simply consider \(\mathbf{x}\) as a collection of \(P \times(L+1)^{2}\) vectors of dimension \(H\), to which equation (11) is applied separately. In other words, ERoPE is applied along the 'feature axis' (of size \(H\) ), whereas the 'parity axis' (of size \(P\) ) and the 'degree axis' (of size \((L+1)^{2}\) ) are treated as ‘batch dimensions’.

Spherical harmonics. The spherical harmonics are functions defined on the surface of the unit sphere \(S^{2}:=\left\{\mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^{3}:\|\mathbf{u}\|=1\right\}\). We follow the conventions used in ref. 41 and define them as real and vector-valued, that is, \(\mathbf{Y}_{\ell}: S^{2} \mapsto \mathbb{R}^{2 \ell+1}\). For example, the first two of these functions (using the Racah normalization convention) are given by \(\mathbf{Y}_{0}(\mathbf{u})=1\) and \(\mathbf{Y}_{1}(\mathbf{u})=\mathbf{u}\). In general, the individual components of \(\mathbf{Y}_{\ell}\) are polynomials of degree \(\ell\) in the \(x, y\), and \(z\) components of the vector \(\mathbf{u}\) (see ref. 41 for a general definition for arbitrary degrees \(\ell\) ).

Lebedev quadrature. A Lebedev quadrature \({ }^{65}\) is a numerical approximation to the surface integral of a function \(f\) over the unit sphere \(S^{2}\)
\[
\int_{S^{2}} f(\mathbf{u}) \mathrm{d} \mathbf{u} \approx 4 \pi \sum_{j=1}^{G} \lambda_{j} f\left(\mathbf{u}_{j}\right),
\]
where \(\mathbf{u}_{j} \in S^{2}\) are grid points and \(\lambda_{j} \in \mathbb{R}\) are the corresponding quadrature weights. The grid can be made denser, that is, the number of grid points \(G\) can be increased, to make the approximation more accurate. Similar to one-dimensional Gaussian quadratures, Lebedev quadratures have the important property that integration of polynomials up to a certain degree is exact. For example, a grid with just \(G=6\) points integrates polynomials of up to degree3 (such as the spherical harmonics with \(\ell \leq 3\) ) without error \({ }^{65}\).

Implementation of EFA. Given atomic features \(\mathbf{x}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times 2 \ell+1 \times H}\), queries, keys and values are calculated as
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{q}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} & =\mathbf{x}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} W_{q}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}, \\
\mathbf{k}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} & =\mathbf{x}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} W_{k}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}, \\
\text { and } \mathbf{v}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} & =\mathbf{x}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} W_{v}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Here, \(W_{q}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_{q k}}, W_{k}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_{q k}}\) and \(W_{v}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times D_{v}}\) are separate weight matrices for each combination of degree \(\ell\) and parity \(p\) and matrix multiplication is performed along the 'feature axis', for example, \(\mathbf{x}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} W_{v}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{P \times 2 \ell+1 \times D_{v}}\). Atomic positions are encoded into query and key vectors with ERoPE
\[
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{\mathbf{q}}_{m, \mathbf{u}}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} & :=\operatorname{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}\left(\mathbf{r}_{m}, \boldsymbol{\Psi}\left(\mathbf{q}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}\right)\right), \\
\tilde{\mathbf{k}}_{m, \mathbf{u}}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)} & :=\operatorname{ERoPE}_{\mathbf{u}}\left(\mathbf{r}_{m}, \boldsymbol{\Psi}\left(\mathbf{k}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}\right)\right),
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}\) can be any feature map that preserves equivariance (see Supplementary equation (5)). Here, we choose a simple identity function or gated \({ }^{67}\) GELU \({ }^{68}\) nonlinearity, which is applied element-wise along the 'feature axis' of size \(D_{q k}\).

The individual \(\tilde{\mathbf{q}}_{m, \mathbf{u}}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}, \tilde{\mathbf{k}}_{m, \mathbf{u}}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}\), and \(\mathbf{v}_{m}^{\left(\ell_{p}\right)}\) for all of the \(N\) atoms are concatenated row-wise to give
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times P_{q k} \times\left(L_{q k}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{q k}}, \\
& \tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times P_{q k} \times\left(L_{q k}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{q k}}, \\
& \mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times P_{v} \times\left(L_{v}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{v}} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Note that queries \(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) and keys \(\tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) must have the same maximal degree \(L_{q k}\), parity size \(P_{q k}\) and feature dimension \(D_{q k}\), whereas the corresponding choices for the values \(\mathbf{V}\) may be different. In addition, we include \(\mathbf{u}\) as a subscript for \(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) and \(\tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) to make explicit that their entries depend on the choice of \(\mathbf{u}\) used for the ERoPE encoding of the atomic positions.

Using slight abuse of notation (which is motivated below), we can now 'translate' the linear-scaling attention-like mechanism introduced in equation (4) to
\[
\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{u}}:=\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}\left(\tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}}^{\top} \mathbf{V}\right),
\]
where \(\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times P_{v} \times\left(L_{v}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{v}}\). Here, the 'outer product' \(\tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}}^{\top} \mathbf{V}\) of keys and values can be computed with time complexity \(\mathcal{O}(N)\) and should be thought of as evaluating to an array of shape \(\left[P_{q k} \times\left(L_{q k}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{q k} \times P_{v} \times\right. \left.\left(L_{v}+1\right)^{2} \times D_{v}\right]\). The subsequent multiplication with \(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) from the left side should be understood as reducing over (element-wise multiplication followed by summation) the last three axes of \(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}\) and the first three axes of \(\tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}}^{\top} \mathbf{V}\). This operation also has a time complexity of \(\mathcal{O}(N)\), which makes the full operation (equation (13)) scale linearly in the number of atoms. We use the suggestive 'matrix-like' notation in equation (13), because when \(P_{q k}=P_{v}=1\) and \(L_{q k}=L_{v}=0\) (special case for 'ordinary' invariant features), many axes of the involved arrays have size 1 and can be omitted ('squeezed') for simplicity. In this special case, \(\tilde{\mathbf{Q}}_{\mathbf{u}}, \tilde{\mathbf{K}}_{\mathbf{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times D_{q k}}\) and \(V \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times D_{v}}\), so equation (13) is correctly described with conventional notation for matrix operations.

The full equivariant EFA update (equation (5)) is implemented as
\[
\operatorname{EFA}(X, R)=\sum_{j=1}^{G} \lambda_{j} \mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{u}_{j}} \stackrel{L_{v} L_{Y}}{\Theta_{\text {out }}} \mathbf{Y}_{\mathbf{u}_{j}}
\]
where we replace the integral over \(S^{2}\) with a Lebedev quadrature and \(\mathbf{Y}_{\mathbf{u}} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 1 \times\left(L_{Y}+1\right)^{2} \times 1}\) is the concatenation of spherical harmonics vectors \(\mathbf{Y}_{\ell}(\mathbf{u})\) up to maximal degree \(L_{Y}\) with additional 'dummy axes' of size 1 that are broadcasted over the corresponding axes of \(\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{u}}\), so that the tensor product contractions (see equation(10)) are well defined. When \(L_{Y}=0\) (and \(\mathbf{B}_{\mathbf{u}_{j}}\) contains only invariant information, that is, \(P_{v}=1\) and \(L_{v}=0\) ), the invariant form of EFA (equation (3)) is recovered.

Numerical accuracy. Because the integration over \(S^{2}\) is performed numerically in equation (14), the accuracy of the rotational invariance/ equivariance of the EFA update is directly related to the precision of the Lebedev quadrature. To better understand the limitations of this approach, we point out that the function that is integrated in equation (14) basically corresponds to a linear combination of terms of the form \(\sin \left(\omega_{k} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{m n}\right) \cdot\) poly \(_{\ell}(\mathbf{u})\) and \(\cos \left(\omega_{k} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{m n}\right) \cdot\) poly \(_{\ell}(\mathbf{u})\), where poly \(_{\ell}(\mathbf{u})\) is some polynomial of degree \(\ell \leq L_{Y}\) in the \(x, y\) and \(z\) components of the vector \(\mathbf{u}\) (the sine/cosine components stem from ERoPE, whereas the polynomials come from the spherical harmonics). As mentioned earlier, polynomials up to a certain degree are integrated exactly when using Lebedev quadratures. While, the sine/cosine components are not polynomials, it is still possible to estimate a sort of 'pseudo-degree' for them: we can approximate these terms by a Taylor series in
\(b:=\omega_{k} \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{r}_{m n}\) around zero. Clearly, the larger \(b\) is, the more terms are required in the Taylor series for a good approximation, which corresponds to a larger pseudo-degree. The largest ‘total degree’ of the terms in the integral is then given by the sum of the highest degree \(L_{Y}\) of the spherical harmonics and the estimated pseudo-degree of the sine/ cosine terms. Assuming a fixed budget of grid points \(G\), the integration will be sufficiently accurate up to some \(b_{\text {max }}\), which yields the inequality \(\omega_{k} r_{n m} \leq b_{\text {max }}\). This can be used to define a maximal frequency
\[
\omega_{\max }=\frac{b_{\max }}{r_{\max }}
\]
up to which (almost) exact rotational invariance/equivariance is ensured. Here, \(r_{\text {max }}\) is the maximal expected distance between atoms (which can be estimated from the training data), and the value of \(\omega_{\text {max }}\) can be chosen accordingly. Alternatively, the number of grid points \(G\) can be increased to make \(b_{\text {max }}\) larger, which in turn allows choosing a larger \(\omega_{\text {max }}\). Because the analytic solution of the integral is known (see 'Analytic solution of the surface integral' section in Supplementary Information), the value of \(b_{\text {max }}\) (for a given \(G\) ) can be easily determined numerically. In this work, we define \(b_{\text {max }}\) as the largest value for which the absolute deviation between numerical and analytic solution stays below a value of \(10^{-5}\) (which roughly corresponds to the numerical precision of single precision floating point arithmetic). We confirm empirically that our implementation of EFA (equation (14)) is rotationally invariant/equivariant up to the desired numerical precision in Supplementary Fig. 5 and provide precomputed values for \(b_{\text {max }}\) as function of \(G\) in Supplementary Table 4.

It should be pointed out that the total cost of evaluating EFA scales as \(\mathcal{O}(N \cdot G)\), that is, it is linear in both the number of particles \(N\) and the number of grid points \(G\). Thus, a computational advantage over quadratically scaling methods is obtained only if \(G<N\). For this reason, \(G\) should be kept as small as possible for maximum efficiency. For many applications, a more lenient accuracy threshold than \(10^{-5}\) (and therefore smaller \(G\) than those listed in Supplementary Table 4) may be acceptable. Empirically, we find that many long-range interactions can be captured accurately with low values of \(\omega_{\text {max }}\), so small grids are typically feasible. For maximum performance, we recommend benchmarking different settings to determine an optimal trade-off between accuracy and efficiency ( \(G\) can even be modified after model training, as it affects only the accuracy of the numerical integration and not the learned interactions). Finally, we note that EFA only scales linearly with respect to \(N\) under the assumption that \(G\) can be kept constant when increasing the number of particles. In cases where an increase of system size also necessitates increasing \(b_{\text {max }}\) (see equation (15)), \(G\) needs to be increased accordingly to keep the same numerical accuracy.

\section*{Neural network implementation}

For the experiments performed in this work, we choose an equivariant MPNN in the spirit of tensor field networks \({ }^{69}\) or NequIP \({ }^{30}\). This base model is then optionally augmented with EFA blocks, and we compare the performance of the different architectural variants. Because our analyses do not depend on particular model details, but rather investigate general limitations inherent to all strictly local MLFFs, we expect our results to also transfer to other choices of local models (which can be augmented with EFA blocks in the same manner). MPNNs are chosen merely as a representative local model due to their popularity, and because an effective cutoff (extending beyond the local cutoff) exists only for MPNN-like models and must be carefully considered when analysing the results (other local models, such as Behler-Parrinello neural networks \({ }^{7}\), can be regarded as an MPNN-like model with \(T=1\) in our considerations).

For the implementations of equivariant operations, we use the E3x library \({ }^{41}\), which is built on top of JAX \({ }^{70}\) and FLAX \({ }^{71}\). NumPy \({ }^{72}\) and ASE \({ }^{73}\) are used for data pre- and postprocessing.

MPNNs. We follow the typical design of MPNNs discussed in the ‘Limitations of local models' section in Main and iteratively update initial embeddings using repeated application of
\[
x^{[t+1]}=\text { MessagePassing }\left(x^{[t]}, \mathcal{R}\right)
\]
(the MessagePassing block is described below). The atomic representations with \(\ell=0\) and even parity are initialized via learned atom type embeddings dependent on the atomic numbers \(z:=\left\{z_{1}, \ldots, z_{N} \mid z_{m} \in \mathbb{N}_{+}\right\}\). In case of the charged cluster experiments (see 'Idealized systems' section), we instead use the atomic charges \(\mathcal{Q}:=\left\{q_{1}, \ldots, q_{N} \mid q_{m} \in \mathbb{Z}\right\}\) to assign the embeddings. All other degree and parity channels are always initialized to zero with the exception of the charge-dipole experiment, where we use the atomic dipole vector with additional parity and feature axes of size 1 (that is, \(\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 3 \times 1}\) ) to initialize the features with \(\ell=1\) and odd parity as \(\mathbf{x}^{\left(\ell_{-}\right)}=\boldsymbol{\mu} W\left(W \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times H}\right.\) is a learnable weight matrix that maps the dipole vector to the feature space).

The \(\ell=0\) components with even parity (invariant parts) of the final atomic features \(X^{[T]}\) are used to predict per-atom energies, which are summed to give the total energy
\[
E_{\mathrm{ML}}=\sum_{m=1}^{N} w^{\top} \mathbf{x}_{m}^{[T]\left(0_{+}\right)}+E_{m, \text { bias }}
\]
where \(w \in \mathbb{R}^{H}\) is a trainable vector, \(E_{m, \text { bias }} \in \mathbb{R}\) is a trainable atom-type-dependent energy bias and \(\mathbf{x}^{[T]\left(0_{+}\right)} \in \mathbb{R}^{H}\) is the invariant part of the final features at layer \(T\) (parity and degree axes of size 1 are omitted here for clarity). In the presence of atomic dipoles, the invariant part is obtained via a tensor product that maps the atomic features of all degrees to invariant features. Forces are calculated as the negative gradient of the energy with respect to the atomic positions \(\left(\mathbf{F}_{m}=-\nabla_{\mathbf{r}_{m}} E\right)\), which can be done efficiently using automatic differentiation.

Equivariant MP block. Throughout this work, we use a prototypical equivariant MPNN. It is built around equivariant continuous convolutions, which are used in many state-of-the-art MPNNs such as NequIP \({ }^{30}\), TFN \({ }^{69}\) and Equiformer \({ }^{42}\). The message sent from atom \(n\) to atom \(m\) is given by
\[
\mathbf{m}_{m n}^{[t]}=\left(\mathbf{W}\left(r_{m n}\right) \circ \mathbf{Y}\left(\hat{r}_{m n}\right)\right) \stackrel{L_{Y} L_{x}}{\otimes} \mathbf{x}_{n},
\]
where \(\mathbf{W}\left(r_{m n}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times\left(L_{Y}+1\right)^{2} \times H}\) is a learned radial filter function containing \(H\) features for each degree \(\ell\) up to \(L_{Y}\). Importantly, the 'subslices' of size \(2 \ell+1\) for each degree \(\ell\) are constrained to contain identical entries (otherwise the operation would not preserve equivariance). The spherical harmonics \(\mathbf{Y}\left(\hat{r}_{m n}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times\left(L_{Y}+1\right)^{2} \times 1}\) use 'dummy axes' of size 1 (similar to the use of spherical harmonics in equation (14)) and the element-wise multiplication '。' is broadcasted along these axes. The messages from all neighbours \(n\) in the neighbourhood \(\mathcal{N}_{m}\) of atom \(m\) (containing all atoms that satisfy \(r_{m n}<r_{\text {cut }}\) ) are aggregated as \(\mathbf{m}_{m}^{[t]}=\sum_{n \in \mathcal{N}_{m}} \mathbf{m}_{m n}^{[t]}\). Note that the radial filter \(\mathbf{W}\left(r_{m n}\right)\) in equation (18) is enforced to smoothly decay to zero at \(r_{\text {cut }}\), so that the learned representations vary continuously when atoms enter or leave \(\mathcal{N}_{m}\). Finally, the updated atom representations are obtained as
\[
\mathbf{x}_{m}^{[t+1]}=\operatorname{MLP}\left[\mathbf{x}_{m}^{[t]}+\mathbf{m}_{m}^{[t]}\right],
\]
where MLP is a multilayer perceptron with two equivariant dense layers and gated SiLU \({ }^{74}\) nonlinearity.

For standard MPNNs, each MP update consists of an equivariant MP block. For an MPNN augmented with EFA, the atomic representations are passed to the equivariant MP block and to the EFA block (see below) and their outputs are added afterwards (see also Supplementary Fig. 4).

EFA block. Non-local atomic representations are calculated using the EFA update (equation (14)), such that
\[
\mathbf{m}_{m, \mathrm{nl}}^{[t]}=\mathrm{EFA}\left(\mathcal{X}^{[t-1]}, \mathcal{\mathcal { K }}\right)_{m}
\]

As for the MP block, per-atomic embeddings are refined via an equivariant MLP, such that
\[
\mathbf{x}_{m, \mathrm{nl}}^{[t+1]}=\operatorname{MLP}\left[\mathbf{x}_{m}^{[t]}+\mathbf{m}_{m, \mathrm{nl}}^{[t]}\right]
\]

\section*{Training}

Models are trained by minimizing a combined loss of energy and forces
\[
\begin{aligned}
L & =\frac{\lambda_{E}}{B} \sum_{b=1}^{B}\left(E_{b, \text { true }}-E_{b, \mathrm{ML}}\right)^{2} \\
& +\frac{\lambda_{F}}{B} \sum_{b=1}^{B} \frac{1}{N_{b}} \sum_{m=1}^{N_{b}}\left\|\mathbf{F}_{m, \text { true }}-\mathbf{F}_{m, \mathrm{ML}}\right\|_{2}^{2}
\end{aligned}
\]
where \(B\) is the number of molecules per batch, \(N_{b}\) is the number of atoms for molecule \(b\) and \(\lambda_{E}\) and \(\lambda_{F}\) are scaling parameters for the energy and force components of the loss.

We use Adam \({ }^{75}\) for parameter optimization (as implemented in the optax \({ }^{76}\) library) and an initial learning rate of \(10^{-3}\). The learning rate is decayed to \(10^{-5}\) at the end of training via an exponential decay schedule. In Supplementary Table 2, we report the full information on optimization settings for each experiment.

\section*{Model hyperparameters}

If not stated otherwise, default EFA blocks use a query and key dimension of \(D_{q k}=16\) and a value dimension of \(D_{v}=32\). The number of Lebedev grid points is set to \(G=50\). The maximal atomic separation varies between datasets and is reported in Supplementary Table 2. For simplicity, we assume the degrees for queries, keys and values to always be equal and define the shorthand \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=L_{q k}=L_{v}\). Other model hyperparameters are described below.

Geometric expressiveness. For the \(\boldsymbol{k}\)-chains experiment, we use the equivariant MP block and the EFA block as standalone component. For the MP block, we follow the original publication \({ }^{45}\) and use a hard cutoff of \(r_{\text {cut }}=10 \AA\) for simplicity. For the EFA block, we use the hyperparameters outlined above. The final output consists of two values that are passed through softmax to predict a probability for each class. The models are trained by minimizing the cross-entropy to the true class labels. The number of features for both blocks is \(H=32\), and the maximal degree in the MP block is \(L_{\text {max }}=2\). The number of MP updates is increased until the model is able to correctly classify the graphs. Because the maximal separation \(r_{\text {max }}\) between atoms increases with \(k\), the maximal frequency for ERoPE (see equation (15)) must be decreased accordingly, leading to slower training convergence for the longest \((k=8)\) chain. Based on theoretical analysis of positional encoding techniques \({ }^{77}\), we can speed up convergence by increasing \(b_{\text {max }}=2 \pi\) and adjust the number of Lebedev grid points to \(G=86\) accordingly (Supplementary Table 4). For the experiments probing distinguishability of neighbourhoods, we use an EFA update as described in equation (5) and produce per degree invariants by taking the L2-norm per degree in the equivariant representation \(\mathbf{x}\) of the central node. We compare the values for each degree \(\ell\) for each pair of distinct neighbourhoods and label the smallest degree for which the invariants are different.

Two-particle systems. For the two-atom systems with a pairwise potential, we use a feature dimension of \(H=128\); a maximal degree of \(L_{\mathrm{MP}}=1\) is used in the MP block and \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=0\) and \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}=\) GELU in the EFA block. The spherical harmonics vector has maximal degree \(L_{Y}=0\) for all models expect for the one applied to the charge-dipole system, for which we additionally train a model with \(L_{Y}=1\) to show that the task can only be solved by including directional information (see main text).

N -particle system. For the \(N\)-atom NaCl -like systems we use a feature dimension of \(H=32\), maximal degrees of \(L_{\mathrm{MP}}=0\), and between \(T=1\) and \(T=5 \mathrm{MP}\) layers (see main text). For models with EFA, we use \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=0\), \(G=194, \omega_{\max }=\frac{2 \pi}{15}, D_{q k}=128, D_{v}=8\) and \(\Psi=\) Id as hyperparameters. Despite the systems having different maximal separation (sphere diameter), we use the same value of \(\mathrm{r}_{\text {max }}=30 \AA\) for all systems to maintain identical maximal frequency (see equation (15)).

Non-local charge transfer. For the non-local charge transfer benchmark, we distinguish between systems with and without PBCs. For the latter, we use \(T=3 \mathrm{MP}\) layers and a local cutoff of \(r_{\text {cut }}=4 \AA\), a feature dimension of \(H=128\) and \(L_{\mathrm{MP}}=2\). For the model with EFA, we use \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=1\) and \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}=\) GELU (other hyperparameters are set to the default values described above). We do not use the EFA block in the final layer as we found this to improve training stability and final accuracy.

For the periodic system, we use \(T=2 \mathrm{MP}\) layers (all other hyperparameters for the MP model are the same as for the non-periodic systems). For EFA, we follow the protocol for PBCs described in 'Other symmetrisation operations' section in Supplementary Information and choose a maximal frequency of \(\omega_{\max }=\pi / 4 \mathrm{in}\) ERoPE with \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=0\) and \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}=\) GELU.

Materials. For the models trained on the materials data from ref. 50, we use the same model as for the periodic system in the non-local charge transfer benchmark (see above).

Molecular systems. For the molecular systems, we use \(T=2\) for the \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) experiments and \(T=3\) for the dimer and cumulene results. The local cutoff is varied between \(r_{\text {cut }}=5 \AA\) and \(r_{\text {cut }}=10 \AA\) for the \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) experiments as described in the main text. We choose \(r_{\text {cut }}=4 \AA\) for the dimers and for cumulene. The feature dimension for the models with EFA block is \(H=128\) for \(T=2\) and \(H=64\) for \(T=3\). For models without EFA block, the feature dimension is increased to \(H=162\) for \(T=2\) and \(H=84\) for \(T=3\), such that models with and without EFA block have approximately ( \(\leq 1 \%\) deviation) the same number of parameters. The maximal degree in the MP block is \(L_{\mathrm{MP}}=2\) and \(L_{\mathrm{EFA}}=0\) in the EFA block, except for the cumulene structures where we additionally train models with \(L_{\text {EFA }}=1\) and \(L_{\text {EFA }}=2\) (see main text). The maximal degree of the spherical harmonics vector in the EFA update is always \(L_{Y}=0\). All EFA models use \(\boldsymbol{\Psi}=\) GELU.

\section*{Pairwise potentials}

For the isotropic two-particle systems, the potential is given by
\[
V\left(r_{m n}\right)=\frac{1}{r_{m n}^{3}}-\frac{1}{r_{m n}}
\]
resembling attractive charge-charge interactions in the long-range regime. For the anisotropic two-particle system, we modify the long-range component to mimic charge-dipole interactions: one atom is assigned a 'charge' and the other a unit 'dipole' vector \(\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathrm{R}^{3}\). The long-range potential is then given as
\[
V\left(r_{m n}\right)=\frac{1}{r_{m n}^{3}}-\frac{\cos (\theta)}{r_{m n}^{2}}
\]
where \(\Theta\) is the angle between \(\boldsymbol{\mu}\) and the normalized displacement vector between the atoms \(\mathbf{r}_{m n} / r_{m n}\). The quadratic decay in distance \(r\) reflects the physical behaviour of charge-dipole interactions.

For the \(N\)-particle NaCl -like system, we assume Na to have positive \(q=+1\) and CI to have negative charge \(q=-1\). The long-range interactions are described via a screened Coulomb potential
\[
V\left(r_{m n}\right)=k_{e} q_{m} q_{n} \frac{\operatorname{erf}\left(\alpha r_{m n}\right)}{r_{m n}}
\]
where \(k_{e}\) is the Coulomb constant, erf is the error function and \(\alpha=0.5 \AA^{-1}\) is a scalar screening coefficient. The screening prevents diverging
energy and forces when \(r\) approaches zero and corresponds to the interaction of Gaussian charge densities.

\section*{NaCl data creation}

We randomly place \(N\) atoms within a sphere of diameter \(d\). We consider systems of increasing diameter \(d\), ranging from \(10 \AA\) to \(30 \AA\). We keep the density constant at 0.075 atoms \(\AA^{-3}\), such that the number of atoms \(N\) increases with \(d\). To ensure a physically meaningful distribution of pairwise distances, we require that the distance \(r_{m n}\) is \(\geq\left(R_{m}^{\mathrm{vdW}}+R_{n}^{\mathrm{vdW}}\right) / 2\) for any two atoms \(m\) and \(n\) (we use \(R_{\mathrm{Na}}^{\mathrm{vdW}}=0.95 \AA\) and \(R_{\mathrm{Cl}}^{\mathrm{vdW}}=1.91 \AA\) as van der Waals radii).

\section*{Dimer data subset creation}

The SPICE dataset \({ }^{78}\) serves as starting point for the dimer data. SPICE contains energies and forces for the dimer geometries in the DES370K dataset \({ }^{48}\) recalculated using density functional theory. The resulting dataset has 4,612 entries and covers a variety of biologically relevant dimers. Each dimer consists of two molecules (monomers) that interact with each other via non-covalent (long-range) interactions. However, the generated dataset is unbalanced in the sense that certain monomers appear more often within dimers than others, biasing the model towards a correct description of dimers with these monomers. To alleviate this problem, we create a curated subset of the dimers data that only uses the most frequent nine monomers. This results in a new dataset of 76 dimers. Almost all combinations of monomers present in the original dataset are also present in the curated version ( 76 versus 81 possible combinations).

